  "worked_solution": "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{1-2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{2\\sqrt{1-2y}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-2y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{1-2y}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\sqrt{1-2y}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{1-2y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\sqrt{1-2y}\\sqrt{\\frac{2-2y}{1-2y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\sqrt{1-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {[-\\frac{2}{3}(1-y)^{3/2}]}_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi (-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{28\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
